  "abstract": {
    "value": "The electrocatalytic reduction of nitrate (NO<sub>3</sub>RR) to ammonia presents a viable solution for addressing nitrate pollution and offers an environmentally-friendly, energy-efficient alternative for industrial ammonia synthesis. However, the absence of efficient electrocatalysts impedes its industrial application. In this study, we constructed a porphyrin organic cage (PB-2) through the covalent-bonded self-assembly. Subsequently, metalized porphyrin organic cages, PB-M (M = Co, Ni, Cu), were synthesized <i>via</i> post-modification of PB-2. These PB-M catalysts were utilized to elucidate the reaction pathway and intrinsic structure-performance relationship of the NO<sub>3</sub>RR. Experimental results indicate that PB-Co exhibits the highest activity and ammonia selectivity (FE<sub>NH3</sub> = 95.8 ± 1.06%, NH<sub>3</sub> yield rate = 995.5 ± 28.4 µmol h<sup>-1</sup> mg<sub>cat</sub> <sup>-1</sup>). Theoretical calculations reveal that the d-p orbital hybridization between the Co 3d orbital in PB-Co and the NO<sub>3</sub> <sup>-</sup> 2p orbital is the strongest one. PB-Co possesses a high d-band center of -0.97 eV and high adsorption energies for NO<sub>3</sub> <sup>-</sup>",
    "source": "external_discovery",
    "confidence": 0.98,
    "human_checked": false
  },
